    "gitlens.ai.generateCommitMessage.customInstructions": "always use valid conventional commit messages with scopes. Don´t create separate commits for code format changes (e.g. newline, whitespaces, and similiar).",
    // "gitlens.ai.generateCommitMessage.customInstructions": "# **Git Flow & PR Conventions:**  Use the **develop** branch as the base for all feature work and pull requests. Name branches and PR titles according to git-flow and **Conventional Commits** standards (e.g. `feat(scope): add new X`), and always include the issue key in the PR title. The CI will enforce Conventional Commit formatting on PR titles. PRs should target **develop** and once approved, merge into develop. Releases are prepared on develop and merged to **main** for publication.  | Action          | Convention                                         | |-----------------|----------------------------------------------------| | Branch Naming   | `feature/<short-description>`, `fix/<issue>`, etc. | | Commit Messages | `feat(mod): add feature description #issue`, etc.  | | Pull Requests   | Must include tests, documentation, and changelog,  | |                 | following Conventional Commit Messages as title    |  ---  ## Commit Message Rules (Conventional Commits)  ### 1. The anatomy  ```text <type>[optional scope][!]: <subject> <blank line> [optional body] <blank line> [optional footer(s)] ```  - **type**: one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`. - **scope** (optional): area of the codebase, e.g. `module`, `script`, `installer`, `psgallery`, `ci`, `api`, `ui`. Use kebab-case for multiwords (`session-manager`). - **!** (optional): marks a breaking change (also explain it in body or footer). - **subject**: short imperative summary (no period at end, max ~72 chars). - **body** (optional): motivation, context, tradeoffs; wrap at ~72 chars. - **footer(s)** (optional): references and metadata, one per line, e.g.:    - `Closes #123`, `Refs ABC-456`   - `BREAKING CHANGE: <what changed and migration instructions>`  #### Examples (good)  ```text feat(module): add Connect-Tenant with MFA support  fix(script): handle null ServicePrincipalId in bootstrap.ps1  docs(readme): add examples for Start-Backup job  refactor(session-manager): extract retry logic into helper  perf(api): cache token for 5 minutes to cut requests  test(psgallery): add Pester tests for Install-Module flow  build(ci): publish artifacts only on tagged builds  ci(github-actions): pin actions/checkout@v4  chore: rename default branch to main  revert: revert \"feat(module): add Connect-Tenant with MFA support\" ```  #### Breaking changes (two ways)  ```text feat(module)!: drop support for PowerShell 5.1  BREAKING CHANGE: The module now requires PowerShell 7+. Update your runners and local environments accordingly. ```  or  ```text feat(module): migrate to new configuration schema  BREAKING CHANGE: Config file now uses `Connections` instead of `Profiles`. Run `.\\tools\\migrate-config.ps1` or update manually. ```  ---  ### 2. When to use each type  - `feat`: user-visible new capability (triggers **MINOR** bump). - `fix`: bug fix (triggers **PATCH** bump). - `docs`: README, changelog, comments only. - `style`: formatting, whitespace, no code behavior change. - `refactor`: code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature. - `perf`: performance improvement. - `test`: add/adjust tests, Pester/pytest/etc. - `build`: build system, packaging, versioning, module manifest tweaks. - `ci`: CI configuration (e.g., GitHub Actions, Azure Pipelines). - `chore`: housekeeping (deps update, rename files), no src/test impact. - `revert`: a revert of a previous commit; message should reference the hash.  > Mapping to SemVer: `feat` → **minor**, `fix` → **patch**, `!` or `BREAKING CHANGE` anywhere → **major**.  ---  ### 3. Writing good subjects  - Use **imperative mood**: \"add\", \"fix\", \"update\", not \"added/adding/updates\". - Keep it **concise** and **specific**: mention the \"what\" and (if helpful) the \"where\". - Avoid redundant words like \"issue\", \"bug\", \"problem\"—the type already conveys intent. - No trailing punctuation; avoid emojis.  **Bad:** `fix: fixed bug` **Good:** `fix(installer): correct target path on Windows Server 2019`  ---  ### 4. Bodies that add value  Use the body to explain **why** not just **what**:  - Context: what led to the change? - Approach: key decisions or tradeoffs. - Impact: risks, user actions required. - For PowerShell code, include command examples or before/after snippets.  Example body:  ```text This ensures Connect-Tenant retries transient 429s from Graph. Implements exponential backoff with jitter and caps at 30s total wait.  Before: - Immediate failure on first 429.  After: - Up to 3 retries before surfacing error.  Refs #231 ```  ---  ### 5. Footers & issue references  - Use `Closes #<id>` to auto-close issues when merging to default branch. - Use `Refs <id>` when linking without closing. - Use one footer per line; put them **after** the body.  #### Examples  ```text Closes #742 Refs ABC-99 ```  ---  ### 6. Scopes for this repo (suggested)  Pick from this list to stay consistent:  - `module`, `script`, `installer`, `psgallery`, `docs`, `ci`, `build`,   `api`, `session-manager`, `auth`, `logging`, `telemetry`, `deps`.  If you need a new scope, choose a clear, short, kebab-case term.  ---  ### 7. Quick templates  #### **Feature**  ```text feat(<scope>): <subject>  <body>  <optional footers> ```  #### **Fix**  ```text fix(<scope>): <subject>  <body>  Closes #<issue> ```  #### **Breaking**  ```text <type>(<scope>)!: <subject>  <body explaining the break and migration steps>  BREAKING CHANGE: <short summary> ```  #### **Revert**  ```text revert: revert \"<original header>\"  Reason for revert (if not obvious).  Refs <commit-hash> ```  ---",
    "gitlens.ai.generateCommits.customInstructions": "always use valid conventional commit messages with scopes. Don´t create separate commits for code format changes (e.g. newline, whitespaces, and similiar).",
